  {
   "item_id": "262ac5f2-4fbe-4968-bde7-f5f68821cc59",
   "source_pool_hash": "3237330ef54779bf1f42940cae99f996554bebece2af46195f4a050076e189a1",
   "metadata": {
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "The US tax credit sets, for 2027, 80% of EV battery critical mineral market value sourced domestically, from US free-trade partners or North American recycling; viability is evaluated.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "26c2dfda-3918-44dd-9027-ef9c1d745f94",
      "text": "The US Inflation Reduction Act sets that in 2027, for an electric vehicle to be tax-credit eligible, 80% of the market value of critical minerals in its battery must be sourced domestically, from US free-trade partners or from North American recycling. The viability of the target is evaluated for different battery chemistries."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1c",
    "judges": {
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Quote extended and verbatim in capture 26c2dfda: the 2027 / 80 % / domestic, US free-trade partners, North American recycling sentence plus \"The viability of the target is evaluated for different battery chemistries.\" Value states the target as a setting (\"sets, for 2027\") and says viability is evaluated, no longer a flat requirement. It does not assert \"contested\" (the quote does not say it), so it is within the quote. Current legal status of the credit is unverified from the pool."
     },
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "what_it_changes states the 2027 80 % sourcing target and that viability is evaluated, both in the quote; no consequence (cost, routing, price) is asserted, so nothing lacks a source or analysis label."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "30d68025-cc44-4386-8073-8335a029c25a",
   "source_pool_hash": "df50bc90d61982f66fb192e34401b473ffa12f6a40547a1e696346cc64bc9cc5",
   "metadata": {
    "why_matters": {
     "value": "Sweden is allowed to charge less excise duty on motor fuel for a short period, and the Decision says such temporary reductions can provide rapid relief to households and undertakings by directly lowering end-user prices. For a transport operator that refuels in Sweden, this can mean a lower pump price on those legs for a limited time. The authorisation is limited in time, so it is a temporary fuel-cost input and not a lasting change.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "e5fbc013-cff2-457a-8793-b4c31784a513",
      "text": "Temporary reductions in the taxation of targeted energy products can provide rapid relief to households and undertakings by directly lowering end-user prices."
     }
    },
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "From 1 July 2026 until 30 November 2026, Sweden may apply motor-fuel excise rates up to 2,4 SEK per litre below the EU minimum levels for gas oil and unleaded petrol.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "e5fbc013-cff2-457a-8793-b4c31784a513",
      "text": "Sweden is authorised to apply a reduction of the rates up to 2,4 SEK per litre below the minimum levels of taxation referred to in Article 7 of Directive 2003/96/EC for gas oil and unleaded petrol used as motor fuels. Article 2 This Decision shall apply from 1 July 2026 until 30 November 2026."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1c",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "why_matters rests on the quote: 'Temporary reductions ... can provide rapid relief to households and undertakings by directly lowering end-user prices'; the operator effect is hedged ('can mean a lower pump price') and no surcharge or invoice pass-through is asserted; 'limited in time' rests on Art 2 and recital 10 in the held capture. Note: the value does not say Sweden has applied the reduction (the Decision only authorises it)."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Value stays inside Art 1 and Art 2: Sweden only, gas oil and unleaded petrol as motor fuel, up to 2,4 SEK/l below the Art 7 minimum, 1 July to 30 November 2026, all inside the quote. Gap (not a fail): Art 2 also ends the Decision early if the Council sets new minimum levels; the value does not mention it."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "41b43061-e8cd-4257-be32-8a15c12a1d00",
   "source_pool_hash": "bdfca6698ccc45e99ae2eb3c58e619baa22115af2cf048b56552ace81b529b3b",
   "metadata": {
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "Shipping companies surrender EU ETS allowances for 70% of their 2025 emissions in 2026, then for 100% of their reported emissions from 2027 onwards, up from 40% of 2024 emissions in 2025.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "84e0bc73-1ee2-4645-a6c4-db6341e7ddd2",
      "text": "2025: for 40% of their emissions reported in 2024; 2026: for 70% of their emissions reported in 2025; 2027 onwards: for 100% of their reported emissions."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1c",
    "judges": {
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Quote verbatim in capture 84e0bc73 (EC climate page). 40 % (2025, 2024 emissions), 70 % (2026, 2025 emissions), 100 % (2027 onwards) all in the quote; the 2024/2025 emissions-year reading is confirmed by the next sentence in the capture (first surrender September 2025 for 1 Jan to 31 Dec 2024). 'Shipping companies' is the source's own phrase; the 5,000 GT and 50 % extra-EU limits are held in the brief's sections. Gap (not a fail): the value itself omits them."
     },
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "what_it_changes states the 40 / 70 / 100 % surrender phase-in by year, all in the quote; no pass-through to surcharges or invoices is asserted, so no unsourced consequence."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "48e2b386-4b3c-4f9d-9780-2948b8779a0a",
   "source_pool_hash": "7bbb4de6d093b41cdb95dafc4c0ec870fabb4bb10c84f645c04f8e76ad1be760",
   "metadata": {
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "From 1 January 2026, a reduction in infrastructure or user charges that a Member State gives zero emission heavy-duty vehicles is limited to 75 % compared to the charge for CO 2 emission class 1.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "4eacff8f-5867-4045-ae4c-812612c4aedd",
      "text": "Member States shall vary infrastructure charges and user charges for heavy-duty vehicles in accordance with this Article. Member States shall apply that variation to the sub-groups of heavy-duty vehicles covered by Article 2(1), points (a) to (d), of Regulation (EU) 2019/1242, at the latest two years after the publication of the reference CO 2 emissions for those vehicle sub-groups in the implementing acts adopted in accordance with Article 11(1) of that Regulation. For CO 2 emission classes 1, 4 and 5, referred to in paragraph 2 of this Article, that variation shall apply to the groups of heavy-duty vehicles not covered by Article 2(1), points (a) to (d), of Regulation (EU) 2019/1242, at the latest two years following the publication of the reference CO 2 emissions in implementing acts adopted pursuant to paragraph 7 of this Article, for the relevant group. Where point 5.1 of Annex I to Regulation (EU) 2019/1242 is amended by a Union legislative act in such a way as to cover the reference CO 2 emissions relevant for a group of heavy-duty vehicles, such reference CO 2 emissions shall no longer be determined pursuant to paragraph 7 of this Article but in accordance with point 5.1 of Annex I to that Regulation. Where emission reduction trajectories for groups of heavy-duty vehicles not covered by Article 2(1), points (a) to (d), of Regulation (EU) 2019/1242, are determined by a Union legislative act amending point 5.1 of Annex I to that Regulation, the variations for CO 2 emission classes 2 and 3, as defined in paragraph 2 of this Article shall apply from the date of entry into force of the new emission reduction trajectories. Without prejudice to the reduction of rates provided for in paragraph 3, Member States may provide for reduced rates of infrastructure or user charges, or exemptions to pay infrastructure or user charges for zero emission vehicles of any vehicle group from 24 March 2022 until 31 December 2025. From 1 January 2026, such reductions shall be limited to 75 % compared to the charge applicable to CO 2 emission class 1, as defined in paragraph 2."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1c",
    "judges": {
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Quote extended and verbatim in capture 4eacff8f (Art 7ga(1)); it now opens \"Member States shall vary infrastructure charges and user charges for heavy-duty vehicles\" and ends with the 1 January 2026 / 75 % / class 1 sentence, so \"heavy-duty\", the date, 75 % and class 1 are all inside it. Value no longer reaches vans or cars."
     },
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "what_it_changes states a limit (75 % cap from 1 January 2026 vs class 1 charge) taken from the quoted sentence; it asserts no cause-and-effect, price, surcharge or cost consequence, so no link lacks a source."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": "d86b7acc-2d92-4914-bebc-5c4799cb6343",
   "source_pool_hash": "7d3f50ef1c716533d2556f8b9576461ad6a6f1e4304b95b43cdc3c871f282656",
   "metadata": {
    "why_matters": {
     "value": "The Directive promotes energy efficiency, and one of its rules reaches businesses directly. Member States must ensure that an enterprise with an average annual consumption higher than 10 TJ of energy over the previous three years, and no energy management system, is subject to an energy audit. A freight business is caught only if its own energy use passes that level, and the rule reaches it through its Member State's national law; where it is caught, an energy audit becomes a legal requirement for it.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "23b933cc-8b9a-40c0-a77a-0a116191f619",
      "text": "Member States shall ensure that enterprises with an average annual consumption higher than 10 TJ of energy over the previous three years, taking all energy carriers together, which do not implement an energy management system are subject to an energy audit."
     }
    },
    "what_it_changes": {
     "value": "Member States must ensure that enterprises above the energy-use threshold, with no energy management system, carry out a first energy audit by 11 October 2026 and then at least every four years.",
     "quote": {
      "capture_id": "23b933cc-8b9a-40c0-a77a-0a116191f619",
      "text": "Member States shall ensure that the enterprises referred to in the first subparagraph carry out a first energy audit by 11 October 2026 and that subsequent energy audits are carried out at least every four years."
     }
    }
   },
   "review": {
    "reviewed_by": "second-reader-rb1c",
    "judges": {
     "J05": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "The consequence (energy audit becomes a legal requirement for an enterprise above 10 TJ with no energy management system) is the quoted Art 11(2) sentence itself; 'reaches it through its Member State's national law' follows from 'Member States shall ensure'; no cost or pass-through asserted."
     },
     "J24": {
      "pass": true,
      "evidence": "Both quotes verbatim in capture 23b933cc (Art 11(2)). 10 TJ over three years, all carriers, no EMS, 11 October 2026, every four years: all in the quotes; the freight-business caveat is stated as conditional on its own use passing the threshold. No widening to a party the quote does not bind (duty is on Member States, via enterprises)."
     }
    }
   }
  }
 ]
}
